  "digitized": {
    "source_view": "3d",
    "source_file": "images/usxbl_2026_gp_championship_3d.jpg",
    "method": "The end-on 3D render prints the 10-ft grid on the turf. Grid rows read where they cross the centre line and width lines where they cross two rows give 27 points; a plane homography fitted to them by DLT lands each within 0.93 px. Each bunker is read where its paint meets the turf nearest the camera and mapped back to feet. The field is mirrored across the 50, and a bunker and its twin are read the same way, so their shared offset (half the footprint depth, the lean of a raised edge) cancels: centre = (near - far + 150) / 2. Centre-line bunkers stand on x = 75.",
    "precision": "Near the camera one foot is about 15 px, at the far end about 6. Mirrored pairs are good to about half a foot; read from a render, not a surveyed 2D.",
    "unlabeled": "The render prints no bunker labels and these are not NXL inflatables. Each is named for the nearest standard kind by shape: pyramids MD, cubes and squared blocks T, tall tapered towers MT, the low wide block Wg, the tall slanted block GW, capped cans C, all-red cans Tr, the X GP, the end-line uprights Br, the W-topped uprights MW, beams SB, the wedge on the centre beam SD. Sizes come from the book.",
    "bunker_count": 57,
    "tool": "tools/digitize_usxbl_gp.py",
    "footprints": {
      "source": "layouts/images/nxl_2026_midwest_2d.jpg",
      "book": "layouts/bunkers.json",
      "tool": "tools/bunkerbook.py",
      "note": "Positions are measured off this event's own official 2D on its printed 10-ft grid. Footprints are not: a bunker type is one inflatable and does not change size between events, so every footprint comes from layouts/bunkers.json, measured once off the unlabeled Midwest Open 2D at 31.1 px/ft, where there is no printed grid, no label and no watermark to read a bunker into. Each bunker keeps what this map drew as drawn_w_ft and drawn_h_ft, and off_ft is how far the two disagree.",
